WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.350
My students built a pulse monitor

2
00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:04.450
that showed twelve beats a minute

3
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:05.530
on a healthy volunteer.

4
00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:07.370
The circuit was fine.

5
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.780
They were sampling a fast

6
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.220
signal far too slowly.

7
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.080
Today: how a sensor signal becomes

8
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.860
numbers, why sampling too slowly

9
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.800
invents fake frequencies, and how

10
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.260
many bits you really need.

11
00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.390
An analogue to digital converter

12
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.130
measures a voltage at regular instants.

13
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.520
The sampling rate is how many

14
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.760
measurements per second.

15
00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.810
Between samples, the

16
00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.210
converter sees nothing.

17
00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.950
Shannon's sampling theorem says a

18
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.830
signal with no frequencies above B can

19
00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.290
be rebuilt exactly from samples taken

20
00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.390
faster than two B per second.

21
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.860
Sample slower, and high frequencies

22
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.900
fold down as fake low ones.

23
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.950
That is aliasing.

24
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.980
Mains hum is fifty hertz.

25
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.100
Sampled sixty times a second, it

26
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.320
appears as a slow ten hertz wave.

27
00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.770
Sampled at a thousand, it

28
00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.490
appears correctly as fifty.

29
00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.830
An n-bit converter splits its

30
00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.870
range into two to the n levels.

31
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.130
A ten-bit converter on three point three

32
00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.990
volts has steps of about three millivolts.

33
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.860
Each extra bit halves the step

34
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.040
and adds about six decibels.

35
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.860
Students treat sampling as

36
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:26.880
a software setting.

37
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.850
Show a spinning wheel under a

38
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.410
strobe light, or a film of car

39
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:33.490
wheels turning backwards.

40
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.940
They see aliasing before

41
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.860
they see the maths.

42
00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:38.610
Quick check!

43
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.230
A vibration sensor must capture

44
00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.210
frequencies up to four hundred hertz.

45
00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.030
What is the lowest sampling rate

46
00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.170
that can work in theory?

47
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.880
Just above eight hundred

48
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:50.940
samples per second.

49
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.220
In practice engineers sample several

50
00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.180
times faster and put an anti-aliasing

51
00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.680
filter in front of the converter.

52
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.210
In the app, open the converter lab.

53
00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.650
Change the sampling rate and the

54
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.890
number of bits, and watch the rebuilt

55
00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.170
signal improve or fall apart.

56
00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:11.270
Your turn.

57
00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.530
A twelve-bit converter has a reference

58
00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.810
of four point zero nine six volts.

59
00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.440
What is the size of one step?

60
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.410
One millivolt, because two to the twelve

61
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.590
is four thousand and ninety six.

62
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.020
Remember: sample faster than twice

63
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.340
the highest frequency, filter first,

64
00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:33.680
and each bit halves the step.

65
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.300
Try it in your next lab: have pairs

66
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.400
sample a signal generator at five

67
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.980
rates on a microcontroller.

68
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.700
They plot what they see and predict

69
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.940
each alias before measuring it.
